Scheduled jobs
gettingstarted-04-scheduled-jobs
Each row is a job: an SD command of yours that a Task Scheduler task runs, unattended, as your Windows user, whether or not you are signed in. The columns are its name, its schedule, its command, when it runs next and how its last run ended, as Task Scheduler reports them. The box's title says when jobs are off.
N makes a job and C (or Enter) changes the selected one: a form asks for the name, the schedule (every 5, 10, 15, 20 or 30 minutes, hourly, daily hh:mm, weekly mon..sun hh:mm, monthly 1..28 hh:mm), the command, and your account password, which a job always needs. D deletes the job under the cursor after you type its name; R starts it now, as its task would. Each is shown as its set.job command in a box answered Run or Cancel; Cancel is what Enter chooses.
Jobs are off as delivered. The administrator turns them on with set.jobs.access on (or on the gates page); while they are off nothing can be made or run, and every job is refused at its next run. A run missed while the computer is off is not made up, and jobs run only while SD runs, which its startup task starts at boot.
